\ core::prelude -- the hub. It declares no words of its own; it re-exports the
\ typed core the compiler used to inject into every program, so a consumer
\ writes one `import: core::prelude * ;` for the whole surface instead of one
\ line per module. Nothing is implicit: a program that wants none of this
\ imports none of it.
\
\ Two of `core::bool`'s names do not appear here. `drop` cannot, at all: it is
\ a gated intrinsic (`import: intrinsics | drop | ;`), not a declared or
\ selectively-imported word, so `export:`'s existence check has no origin to
\ record for it (tracked as P7.S3q). The bool `.` overload cannot either: an
\ operator overload's candidate lookup considers the calling module and the
\ module it selectively imported the name from, one hop, so a hub in between
\ hides the declaring module. A program that calls `drop`, or prints a `Bool`,
\ imports `intrinsics`/`core::bool` directly; everything else comes through
\ here.
import: self::bool | if unless Bool False True | ;
import: self::cmp | eq lt gt lte gte ne | ;

export: if unless Bool False True eq lt gt lte gte ne ;
